# docker-compose — real-world examples

Modern Docker ships this as a plugin: `docker compose ...` (no hyphen)
works the same way.

## Start everything defined in docker-compose.yml

```sh
docker-compose up

# in the background
docker-compose up -d

# from a specific compose file
docker-compose -f <path/to/compose_file.yml> up

# rebuild images first
docker-compose up --build

# only specific services
docker-compose up <container_name_1> <container_name_2>
```

## Status and logs

```sh
# List running containers for this project
docker-compose ps

# Follow logs from everything
docker-compose logs --follow

# Follow logs from one service
docker-compose logs --follow <container_name>
```

## Stop and clean up

```sh
# Stop all running containers
docker-compose stop

# Stop and remove containers, networks, images, and volumes
docker-compose down --rmi all --volumes
```

## Inspect a container's environment

```sh
docker-compose run <container_name> env
```

## Homelab: media stack on mediaserver

```sh
cd /srv/stacks/media
docker-compose up -d               # plex, sonarr, radarr...
docker-compose pull && docker-compose up -d   # update images in place
docker-compose logs --follow plex  # watch Plex start up
```

| Command  | What it does                       |
| -------- | ---------------------------------- |
| up -d    | create + start in background       |
| down     | stop + remove containers/networks  |
| pull     | fetch newer images                 |
| ps       | project container status           |
| restart  | bounce services                    |
| exec     | run a command in a running service |
